Then that would've proven my point: that free speech doesn't always 
succeed in defending free speech, and that only the "fundamentalists"
of civil libertarianism believe otherwise.

 : I might have (with help) sued the univeristy.

But your suit would've defended absolutely no one's (!)
free speech:   UNTIL AND UNLESS: somebody in the EXECUTIVE 
branch sent some GUNS to enforce the Supreme Court's ruling.  
Claiming that speech alone could've staved off the incipient
bloodbath is quite preposterous.  

On the other hand, no one's free speech would've been endangered
by the passage of the pro-censorship rule, either, until and unless
the university sent some GUNS to expel the people it wanted to censor,
so that is not the real debate (the Supreme Court's guns vs. the
university's guns is no longer a free speech issue).  The issue
is properly frameable only in the context of a private university,
or of the national government at the highest level, when there is
no higher authority to appeal to if free speech results in a provably
unfree outcome.

You also haven't addressed the core question: WHICH scenario is
more unfree?  The one in which bigoted students are free to
foster an OVERALL campus climate that is hostile toward the
free expression of those in the democratic minority (i.e.,
those who lost the election 80% to 20%), or the one in which
the speakers whose speech would be root causes of the prevalence
of that social dynamic (who might themselves only be 10% of the
voters) are censored from spouting harassing and denigratory bullshit?


   ========== ftp.eff.org:pub/academic/law/brandenburg-v-ohio =============
   Is there a right to speech that advocates illegal acts or violence?

[ deleted  long & tragic history of Supreme Court failure to uphold the
