The fact that this is the law has precious little to do with whether it
is good or bad.  Moreover, what is appropriate for the government is
not necessarily appropriate for the academy.  In case you hadn't figured
this out yet, most voters do NOT have college degrees.  Most of the people
WE are talking about governing DO HAVE or are trying to have them.
It DOES MAKE a difference.

 :   Here's what Alan Dershowitz writes about the distinction between advocacy
 :   and incitement (_The Best Defence_ p. 222):
 :   "Advocacy is the communication of ideas; it is directed at intellect;
 :   it affords the listener an opportunity to reflect on it.  Incitement,
 :   on the other hand, ... is a spur to automatic action, intended to
 :   bypass the rational thought processes. 

This thread originally came out of soc.culture.african.american 
and alt.discrimination on the subject of black colleges.  It only
later spilled over into alt.politics.correct and alt.comp.acad-freedom.
If you had seen the articles that originally motivated this, then
you would've known that they were properly classed as incitement
by this definition, as is most of what Leonard Jeffries wanted to
teach about Jews (fuck the New York courts).

  : It is against this oversimplified background that the classic 
  : case of shouting fire in a crowded theater can best be understood.
  :  Shouting the word "fire" is not the communication of an idea 
  : designed for reflective thought; it is precisely the same as if 
  : a fire *bell* were intentionally rung. It is intended to spur 
  : an automatic series of responses."

This is tautologous and therefore uninformative.
The purpose of MOST speech is to motivate ACTION,
NOT mere reflection.  If it motivates reflection, then the
purpose of the reflection is usually so that BETTER action
can be taken thereafter.

Everything Rush Limbaugh, Pat Robertson, or Pat Buchanan
has relevantly said falls into the category of inciting
immediate action based on fear as opposed to reflective
thought in support of eventual action based on rationality.
EVERYTHING.  That is American conservatives' WHOLE strategy.
That doesn't really bother me because free speech is a good
vehicle for exposing that fact.  What bothers me is the
fact that in SMALLER communities, SOCIAL norms can develop
which are much more effectively restrictive of people's
behavior than the government edicts you are talking about
deterring here.  What bothers me is the failure of so many
people within the academy to understand what an academy is
and why it's there.

