In article <CHttrs.GKI@cs.uiuc.edu> kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:
>>But it's up to the owner of the copyright to do something about it.
>
>Hear, hear! It would be very easy for Playboy to stop postings of
>their pictures.

And it would not even be that hard for those violating copyrights to
call up Playboy and ask them.   They are in Chicago.

Carl, you misunderstand the law here.  It is not Playboy's duty to stop
the piracy.  Rather, it is the duty of those posting Playbody's material
to call or write Playboy and ask permission.

And the fact is, I expect everybody here knows what Playbody's answer
would be.  Which makes this worse.  If you know they will say no, and you
don't ask them because you know this, you're in a worse position.

The statements above are ludicrous.  Failure to sue or threaten are not
at all the same thing as granting permission!   Playboy did sue a
particularly blatant case -- that's the way lawsuits work, they usually
only apply to the blatant cases you can get money from.

>
>Note that fair use copying is "Posted without permission" but does not
>infringe on copyright. In other words, "Posted without permission" is
>not necessarily mark a copyright violation.

But it almost always is here on USENET.   Generall, the following
items characterize "fair use."

a) The amount quoted is short, usually an excerpt of a larger work.

b) It should be attributed.

c) It should be posted for the purpose of commentary, criticism, parody
education, or reporting the news.   In the latter case, the *writing itself*
must be the news, not the things reported.  (ie. you can't reproduce a story
from the New York Times because you are reporting the same news.  Only
if the *story itself* is news -- rather than the subject of the story being
news -- does this case apply.)

d) Your goal must be to talk about the creative expression quoted or
used, not to exploit it.


The last part really sums it all up.  Ask, "could I have rewritten this
in my own words, and why didn't I?"   If the answer is that you could, and
the only reason you didn't was to avoid the labour, then it is not fair use.
