We've dealt with that problem at the school level on a couple of occasions. 
I've been working for a couple of years with Selma Middle School, Selma, IN,
and now with Burris Laboratory School here at Ball State on making Internet
accessible to school-age children.  Our approach has been to *start* with a
committee comprising teachers, parents, school childrent, and school and
community leaders.  We help them to understand the importance of open
information access and the dramatic changes that and information access model
can bring to a school and a community.  From the beginning our assumption has
been that Internet to the school serves not only the teachers and children but
the parent and ultimately the wider community as well.  We have been frank with
the committees as to some of the hazards of Internet and have stressed the need
for parents and teachers to monitor children's use of Internet.  We have also
emphasized,  as a part of the information access model, the need to teach
ethics and responsible behavior in the information age to children and adults
alike.

I would like to think that it is because of the openness with which we have
confronted these issues and the education we have provided to parents and
children that we have had no serious school or community objections to Internet
access.  That is not to say that we haven't had a few children push the limits. 
We have, and where necessary the principals have taken the sorts of
disciplinary measures one would expect them to and the incident has been
closed.

