From: kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie)
Subject: UN's Article 19 coming true despite government efforts
Message-ID: <CHzp4J.A1D@cs.uiuc.edu>
Date: Mon, 13 Dec 1993 20:05:07 GMT

Article 19 of the U.N's International Covenant on Civil and Political
Rights starts out:

   Article 19

   1. Everyone shall have the right to hold opinions without
   interference.

   2. Everyone shall have the right to freedom of expression; this
   right shall include freedom to seek, receive and impart information
   and ideas of all kinds, regardless of frontiers, either orally, in
   writing or in print, in the form of art, or through any other media
   of his choice.

Then the weasel words start:
   
   3. The exercise of the rights provided for in paragraph 2 of this article
   carries with it special duties and responsibilities. It may therefore be
   subject to certain restrictions, but these shall only be such as are
   provided by law and are necessary:

    (a) For respect of the rights or reputations of others;

    (b) For the protection of national security or of public order (ordre
        public), or of public health or morals.
   
The weasel words continue in Article 20:

Article 20

   1. Any propaganda for war shall be prohibited by law.

   2. Any advocacy of national, racial or religious hatred that constitutes
   incitement to discrimination, hostility or violence shall be prohibited by
   law.

But the weasel words don't matter. Sure China can try to stop faxes
and (more recently) satellite TV. Sure the U.S. can try to restrict
the message of Canadian films and Irish speakers. Sure Canada can try
to restrict international TV, radio, cable, newspaper, and Netnews
stories about semi-secret trials. They can try, but they will fail
more and more often.

The governments of the world say that

   "Everyone shall have the right to freedom of expression; this right
    shall include freedom to seek, receive and impart inform and ideas
    of all kinds, regardless of frontiers, except ..."

But technology says that

   "Everyone *does* have the right to freedom of expression; this right
    *does* include freedom to seek, receive and impart inform and
    ideas of all kinds, regardless of frontiers PERIOD"

- Carl
