In article <2ei1a0$3dr@s.ms.uky.edu> kherron@ms.uky.edu (Kenneth Herron) writes:

>OTOH, I don't see this as a rationale for mandating that sites treat
>a.b.p.e just like any other group.  If some institution, educational
>or otherwise, feels it should drop a.b.p.e because of the lawbreaking
>going on, bully for them.

Well, in one case I think that abpe should be treated just like any other
group:  if a site decides to drop abpe because it feels that ALL the
articles posted there are in violation of someone's copyright (which of
course is not true, but let's assume that it is for the moment), they should
also be prepared to drop other groups that have the same problem.  This is
where abpe should be treated as any other group.  Or perhaps, any other
group should be treated as abpe!

But when a site drops abpe, and yet continues to carry other similar groups,
the problem is NOT copyright violations.  The problem is "erotica".  For how
can one claim that abpe is filled with copyright violations and yet miss all
the album covers that are being scanned and posted in the other binary groups?

Is it "fair use" to post the album covers and yet "copyright violations" to
post pictures from magazines?  I think not.

If one is so concerned about copyright violations that abpe must be pulled,
then one better be looking at all such groups.  Otherwise what we're really
talking about is censorship.  "Copyright violations" is a way of not having
to admit that we're uncomfortable with "erotica".

It's interesting that the "defenders of copyrights" always seem to complain
about abpe but never mention abp.misc.  Why is that?  Why is the "erotica"
group always the one under scrutiny?

I believe that the users of abpe really do need to clean up their acts, so
to speak.  But I also feel that the other picture groups (as well as many
of the traditional USENET discussion groups) need to "clean up" just as much!

I believe that abpe is singled out because of its erotic content.  If it
contained pictures taken from "Car and Driver" rather than "Playboy" I doubt
that we'd hear very much from USENET's self appointed "copyright police".

But the copyright discussion leads me to ask:  At what percentage of
"copyright violations" should a group be pulled?  If abpe is pulled because
it is "100% Copyright Violations" would "90%" be acceptable?  How about "30%"?

At what point should groups that have pirated magazine articles be dropped?
Should a single such pirated article cause a group to be dropped?  How about
a dozen?  Where does one draw the line?

I think that respecting the property of other is important.  But I think the
education and enforcement needs to be done at the user level, not the newsgroup
level.  Instead of demanding that groups be removed, why not insist that
posters and sites respect the copyright?

As I said in a previous article, it may not be your responsibility to defend
Playboy's copyright, but there's nothing stopping you from dropping "Hef" a
line and telling him that "Joe Schmoe from Cocomo" is posting one of "Hef's"
pictures.  Let "Hef" take the abuser to task if the user ignores your plea
to stop.

Terry
