greeneg@cs.unc.edu (George Greene) writes:

>You are displaying a tragic lack of understanding of the difference
>between the graduate and the undergraduate halves of a university.
>At the graduate level, OF COURSE you have to have a marketplace of
>ideas.  You are researching things that are not fully understood,
<snip>
>BUT

>At the undergraduate level, things are ENTIRELY different.
>You are teaching what everybody KNOWS already, what is BEYOND
>dispute.  You cannot have anybody who intends to teach that
>the earth is flat in an undergraduate geology department.
>That isn't even a QUESTION of free speech, or of a marketplace
>of ideas.  Undergraduates aren't in school for the purpose of
>buying what they feel like from the market.  They're in
>school because the volume of relevant known facts is so big that 
>they have to be.  It is not possible to question everything

Big problem here. It's philosophical. Teaching facts is a loosing
proposition. Teaching thinking through example is better. 
Controversial subjects are best because you can display the
difference between competing lines of reasons which aren't finished.

Now maybe engineers need to master certain acts of calculaus and the
F=ma stuff. Chemists may need to learn the Grignard reaction and others.
But if one only sees an undergrad education as amassing facts, one
is lost. The point of an undergrad education, it seems to me, ought
to be to teach people how to learn towards the edge of knowledge.
Graduate schools is for pushing on the frontier.

I don't think that this is a trivial distinction. It think it is
fundamental and an aspect of a growing failure of many undergrad
programs. Applied thinking may require some level of facts but the
facts are baggage and not the point. The point is in the skill of
application.

Again, I'll confess that engineering has more of a trade school
manifesto but George's comment was in distinguishing between Grad
and Undergrad overall.

Therefor, I take some exception to an atmosphere of protecting
undergrads from dangerous ideas. That is elitist and counter to
what I see as the proper mission for an undergrad ciriculum.
