In article <CHz9wx.B63@cs.uiuc.edu> kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:
>Actually I belive both duties exist. Everyone has a duty to respect
>Playboy's copyright *and* Playboy has a right to stop those who
>don't respect it copyright.

Carl, you contradict yourself in the same sentence.  We have a duty to
respect copyright.  Playboy has a *right* to enforce their copyright, not
a duty.  You use the right words, but your sentence "both duties" makes
no sense because there is only one duty.

And yes, we don't strictly have a duty to uphold Playboy's copyright.

We do have a duty, however, to ensure that we are not knowing or active
participants in *organized* copyright infringment.  And the fact is that
I can't see an outsider seing the alt.picture groups as anything but
organized infringement, since while other groups only rarely have
violations, in these groups the majority -- almost the entirety -- of the
postings are infringements.

We know this.  We can't pretend we weren't looking or that nobody can examine
every newsgroup.   Of course you can't examine every group, but when you
are told (and you've been told, this debate has come up often enough) the
defense of "I can't look at everything" vanishes.

So at the least, you need to remove any groups that appear to exist
primarily for copyright infringement from your own systems.  Most people
who care have done that.

Do you have a duty beyond that?  That's a harder question.  No, in the
strict sense of duty, you do not.

But do you care about USENET and its existence in the legal world?  Do
you want USENET to be a place for wholesale copyright infringement.  Do you
want the place it will become if it gets that reputation, or the problems
it will have with that reputation?

There are many legit BBSs but the pirate BBSs often give BBSs a bad name.
The same can happen to USENET.

Let me correct that -- already has happened to USENET.  My #1 problem
these days in convincing information providers to let me sell their news
on the net in USENET format is that they've heard there's a lot of piracy.
Many of them think "on the internet" means "available to anybody" or
"to be widely pirated and out of control."  (Odd, when the internet is
actually a point to point network, except for the mbone.)  I'm living with
the reputation problem already.  You can either take that as, "Ah, poor
wittle Bwadley" or "Hmm, that may be the first sign of trouble to come."

Take your pick.
