mathew@unixg.ubc.ca (Mathew Englander) writes:
>> Further British libel law 21-15 states that it is an acceptable
>>defence to argue that the publication was made abroad and is not
>>actionable in that country.

>This of course brings up the question that gets asked on Usenet, oh, every
>five minutes: is a newsgroup posting a publication? The answer: nobody will
>know until the courts decide. But I would hazard to say that if it is a
>publication, it is a publication everywhere in the world that Usenet reaches,
>not just in the jurisdiction where the article was originally keyed in.
>However, I really don't know very much about defamation law.

Well, not quite. The _only_ court opinion that I've come across that's on
point splits some awfully fine hairs. The key here is _who is liable_, not
_whether there is a publication_.

	The original poster is probably liable as a publisher wherever the
	message could be "reasonably foreseen" as going. This is true under
	all common-law schemes. In the US, see Keeton v. Hustler Magazine,
	549 A.2d 1187 (1988), which contains a fairly good discussion of the
	rules for publication under R2d (Torts). I don't have them in front
	of me, but statutes define the scope of publication in the UK, Canada,
	and Australia; a couple of law review articles on comparative tort
	law have made farther-reaching statements about common-law publication.

	The Usenet itself is not liable in the US, since it is a distributor,
	not a publisher. See Cubby, Inc. v. CompuServe, Inc., 776 F. Supp. 135
	(S.D.N.Y. 1991), which has been favorably cited in several other 
	states. In Cubby, the court found that NY law treated CompuServe as a
	distributor, not a publisher (the target was contractual reposting of
	material from Consumer Reports, if I remember correctly).

	Usenet operators (e.g., the good folks at Panix) are almost certainly
	not liable as publishers, _so long as they do no editing_. And, on a
	moderated newsgroup like this one, that could lead to some really
	high legal fees!

And, as usual, anyone who treats this as legal advice probably believes in the
Tooth Fairy, the Easter Bunny, Santa Claus, and honest politicians.


