I've been lurking on this group on and off, usually in hope of seeing
some constructive comments on politics throughout the mideast -- not just
disputes involving Israel, and certainly not gratuitous insults thrown
by one side against the other.

I don't agree with much of what Mr. Sirhan has been saying.  I think
it's an entirely legitimate point that the Palestinians have suffered
many wrongs at the hands of the Israelis and that they are very justified
in presenting grievances.  Mr. Sirhan illustrates this sense of grievance
very well; it's just that the main problem I have with his postings is
that he offers no solutions on how to *resolve* these grievances.  He
seems to think that if Islamic states somehow find enough cohesion to
threaten Israel, collectively, then Israeli may grant certain concessions.
In fact, Israeli is likely to perceive a threat and react violently --
as it has a history of doing, and IMHO, overdoing.

OK, so those are my biases -- somewhat pro-Palestinian, to be sure, but
it's only fair that I let everyone know about them.

The above posters tells us that he has sent copies of Mr. Sirhan's
"inflammatory" postings to the FBI and INS.  I vociferously oppose this
move.  Why?  Well, here in the United States, we have something called
free speech, and merely making an inflammatory or offensive posting is
not a legitimate reason, per se, to deport someone or to inform upon
them to the authorities.  We have a right to free speech in this country.
Now, granted, our free speech rights aren't absolute.  We have no
right to yell "fire" in a crowded theater, for example.  Nevertheless,
Mr. Sirhan, as long as he is in Arizona, enjoys the protection of U.S.
laws, and nowhere in his postings do I find a "clear and present danger"
of inciting violence -- the criterion under which courts have held we
may limit speech.  I don't know whether, in principle, Mr. Sirhan
advocates killing; the jury is still out on that.  We get contradictory
statements such as, "No need to kill anyone" and, to paraphrase, "Palestinians
and Israelis have killed each other; the side that has more deaths should
kill members of the other side until the number of deaths is equalizied."
A horribly offensive thought, I would agree -- but it's *not* a specific
threat against a single individual.  I therefore don't see any clear and
present danger, and therefore I don't think it's legitimate for the
FBI to harass Mr. Sirhan unless they know something we don't.

As for sending Mr. Sirhan's postings to the INS, I see no evidence
that he favors the forcible overthrow of the United States government,
and nor do I see evidence that he is a supporter of the Communist
Party.  I therefore see no basis in law for deporting him, although
I am admittedly not an immigration lawyer.  Perhaps if there are any
immigration lawyers out there, they could inform us of what criteria
might be used.  Now, it might be assumed that Mr. Sirhan advocates
the overthrown of the Israeli government, but I see no requirement
that the INS act on behalf of the Israeli government, but rather on
behalf of U.S laws and U.S. interests alone.

I can further see that Israel might have a less rigid standard for
determining what speech presents a clear and present danger and
can therefore be limited than does the USA.  Setting aside for
the moment the issue of Israeli crimes against Palestinians, Israel
does have security problems the US does not.  However, allow me to point
out that Mr. Sirhan is evidently in Arizona.  Therefore, American standards
for free speech, and not Israeli ones, should be used.

If Israel is truly the democracy it claims to be, then it ought
to respect free speech in general, and it ought to respect American
definitions of clear and present danger as they apply to indivduals in
U.S. borders.  I further note that the above poster evidently lives
in Israel.  Now, I am not one of these Pat Buchanaesque "national
sovereignty" freaks, and in fact I support interdependence agenda
things such as NAFTA.  However, I think it presumptious that an Israeli
should be defining -- for organizations such as the FBI and INS
which fall under American jurisdiction, no less -- what lies under
the purvue of American law and what lies in the American national
interest.

If Mr. Sirhan advocates a specific act of violence, or if he
threatens a specific individual or group by name, then we might
have a clear and present danger.  Until then, we have only
inflammatory and offensive postings, as the above poster stipulates.
Well, I say that even though inflammatory postings may not be
positive contributions to talk.politics.mideast, they are not
a clear and present danger, and therefore coercive action
against Mr. Sirhan is not justified.  Condemn his postings, acknowledge
the grievences of the Palestinians if you fee inclined to do so --
but deny no one free speech if you support democracy.

