In the United States judicial system, in order to bring a case
to court, it is necessary to have what is known as "standing to
sue."  This doctrine has myriad implications, but one of them
is that individuals generally cannot artificially create
some grievance and then sue to have that grievance remedied --
all in the ultimate name of illustrating some broader point.
That is, one cannot bring a contrived test case to court;
one must suffer a bona fide wrong.

I submit that you do not have standing to sue here,
and presumably if all the FBI has to go on is your
information, it (correctly) realizes it does not
yet have standing to sue.

