Being half-way to a terrorist is not being a terrorist.  And justifying
terrorist acts is not a crime, at least not in the United States.
Committing them is, and presenting a clear and present danger of
commiting one is.

And on this point of justifying terrorist acts: is there *no* conflict
in the world today in which the use of political violence is justified?
(I'm not just talking about the Middle East, here.)  If you are a pacifist
and think the use of political violence is never justified, then clearly
terrorist acts can never be justified.  However, it's not a given
that poltical violence is never justified, a George Kennan pointed out
in the book "American Diplomacy."  My personal view is that I think
political violence is rarely, if ever, justified, but I also think
it's important to point out there's no philosophical consensus on this
question.

In any case, advocating that political violence is justified isn't
a crime; only committing political violence is.  By your standards,
we ought to arrest Rabin, Arafat, and the whole lot of them when they
set foot in the United States.

There is no surer way to make terrorism and underground movements
popular than to limit their civil liberties.  So if you want to talk
about prevention, realize you aren't preventing anything by curtailing
civil liberties.  And have *you* ever heard of prior restraint?
