>
>As for Canada: Can a Canadian really lose a suit in a UK court under
>UK law for something that is legal in Canada?

Well, of course. The relevant question is: Can a Canadian really lose a
suit in a UK court under UK law for something that is legal in Canada,
*and have the judgment enforced in Canada*?

The answer to this question is a qualified yes. The Supreme Court of
Canada has not answered this question definitively, but lower-court decisions
seem to be heading toward that answer. (And it's not just the UK;
theoretically it could be a court from any country in the world.) 
The qualification would be that the territory of the original court
had a "real and substantial connection" to the matter being litigated.
The details of this "real and substantial" connection test have yet to be
worked out.

> In email someone told me
>that if this is true, it may be time for Canada to consider
>independence.

Yes, very cute. If the courts do finally decide that foreign judgments
are enforceable in Canada, Parliament (and possibly any provincial
legislature) would be able to supersede the decision by statute.
Canada's independence is in no danger.

