I thought a lot of people other than those on my distribution list might be
interested in the following information on California's loyalty oath.

***IF YOU POST A FOLLOW-UP TO THIS MESSAGE, BE SURE TO EDIT THE "Newsgroups"
LINE DOWN TO SOMETHING REASONABLE, such as just ca.politics or just
alt.society.civil-liberties.

No flames, please; just press the 'n' key.  Anyway at this point I've probably
heard it all before.

--------CUT HERE--------

This is an update on what I've learnt about the State Oath of Allegiance.
You're receiving this message because you're among a group of people who
expressed opposition to the oath either after my posting about it in September
1992 or during the Konishi affair.  This message is being delivered by
blind-carbon-copy in order to maintain confidentiality of all your identities
and e-mail addresses.

Well it has been quite the run-around.  I wrote to state senator Lucy Killea and
assembly member Mike Gotch immediately after my run-in with the oath in
September 1992, and was ignored.  I badgered both of their offices about it by
telephone a couple times since then, and finally succeeded in extracting a
verbal reply from Gotch's office this past September.  Gotch's comment, as
conveyed by his staff, was that mine was an interesting argument but that a
repeal of the oath, which could happen only by amendment of the California
Constitution, would not be politically practical.  I still have had no response
from Killea.  Have any of you written to legislators about the oath, and if so,
what, if any, response did you get?

I also contacted the San Diego office of the ACLU last year.  The person whom I
spoke with by telephone at the time said he knew nothing offhand about the
history of the oath and hadn't the staff to research it, but would search his
files and send me anything about it that he had.  I never heard from him.  This
past month I contacted the national office of the ACLU in New York, and this
time received information from the staff counsel at the San Diego office.  He
sympathised with me but cited the 4-3 decision of the US Supreme Court in _Cole
v. Richardson_, 405 US 676 (1972), as a precedent affirming the
constitutionality of state loyalty oaths.  However, the Massachusetts oath that
was the basis for that decision differed from California's oath in two ways
that I believe may be significant.  Firstly, California's oath contains the
statement 'I take this obligation freely, without any mental reservation or
purpose of evasion'.  Yet, many students _do_ have mental reservations about
this oath and are being coerced into signing it by the threat of discontinuation
their support in graduate school.  The University of California has been luring
prospective students into the state and then hitting them with the oath
requirement only after they've declined other options and enrolled in the UC.
Under such circumstances the decision to sign is not in fact free, and therefore
the declaration 'I take this obligation freely' would perjure the signatory.

The second difference from the Massachusetts case is really a separate
constitutional question.  My sticking point in not signing the oath has been
article XX.19 of the California Constitution, which effectively prevents UC
students from being supported in employed positions if they _speak_ (not
necessarily _act_) in favour of the violent or illegal overthrow of the
government or in favour of supporting an enemy in time of 'hostilities' (not
necessarily war).  This article is clearly a First Amendment violation, yet to
my knowledge it has never been challenged.  Since the State Oath of Allegiance
would require me to support this constitution one of whose articles I believe to
be illegal, I cannot subscribe to it.

I am informed by the ACLU counsel that notwithstanding these differences from
the oath that was subject to question in _Cole v. Richardson_, that decision is
likely to be broadly applied as a precedent, so even if one had the resources
with which to bring a case to court, its chance of being heard and favourably
decided would be meagre.

So we are stuck.  Changing the oath would be politically damaging for the
legislators, so they're not going to touch it.  And given the legal precedent in
favour of the Massachusetts oath, no organisation is likely to commit the
resources necessary for a legal challenge.  It seems that all any of us who
oppose the oath can do is to publicise the issue and to oppose it in our own
small ways.  As for me, especially having invested so much effort in a fight
against it, I would sooner drop out of school than sign the thing.  Since I'm
currently being paid by an off-campus lab and not by UCSD, it hasn't come to
that.  I might be TAing a course during an upcoming quarter, and if that
happens UCSD will probably hit me with the oath and cancel my salary when I
refuse to sign, but I would prefer to live on savings for a few months than to
sell out my honour by subscribing to an oath that I do not believe in.

I'll send another mailing to this distribution list if and when I hear anything
new.  If you would like to be removed from this distribution list, send me
e-mail.  Anyone who would like to be added to this list also should send me
e-mail.  Feel free to re-distribute this message without constraint.  (Just
don't give it to the CIA - they probably have it already anyway. :-)

Matthew Belmonte
mbelmonte@ucsd.edu

