In article <barryCI1Fzq.6I5@netcom.com> barry@netcom.com (Kenn Barry) writes:
>	I'll let Carl cite the precedents, but you're simply wrong,
>Brad. Both copyright and trademark protection can be legally _lost_ if
>the holder consistently fails to pursue violators.


I'm simply wrong, am I?  Before saying that with such certainty, I would
advise trying to find the precedents you refer to, particularly under
the new copyright laws which have come into play recently.

Under these laws, copyright can only be lost if it is explicitly abandoned.
That means you write the words, "I abandon the copyright" or "I place this
in the public domain" on the work or in some public place.

As far as I know, nothing short of this loses you a copyright.  You may
reduce your ability to claim damages by certain actions or inactions, but
only the above explicit actions can lose copyright.

As far as I know, Playboy does neither of these.

Some people confuse this with trademarks, which can be lost due to lack of
enforcement.
