>be exempted; I was considering the large number of publically funded
>universities and colleges (e.g. University of California).
>
>If, however, Stanford is funded even in part by public funds, then they
>too no longer qualify as a "privite institution" for purposes of the
>first amendment.
[...]

Private schools that take public money are not automatically required
to respect Consititutional rights. They may, however, required by
*statute* to respect students rights as a condition of receiving
funds. For example the FERPA requires that privacy rights be
respected. Title IX requires that equal opportunity rights be
respected.

There is talk of a federal law requiring that free expression rights
be respected at any university taking federal funds, but it does not
exist yet. I believe that California does have such a free expression
law.

Also, although most U.S. private universities are not required by law
to respect free expression, they respect it anyway, often via a clause
in the student code. This gives them a contractual obligation.

