Karl Denninger writes [referring to UUNET's newsgroup file one 
line descriptions]

> UUNET is considered a rather authoritative source for these files.  I 
> did not make these descriptions up myself.

In many cases, I think it would be a mistake to take UUNET's one line
descriptions for the alt groups and use them without modification -- 
many of them are written tounge-in-cheek, which is just fine
*unless* they are being used or relied upon by:

   -- a new news user who is already feeling overwhelmed by the
      mechanics of using a newsreader, beginning a kill file, 
      getting a managable subscription list, etc.

      "What kind of description is *this*?" they say...

   -- a news user for whom english is a second language

      "My english is not too good. What is meant by the following.... ?"

   -- a news user from a foreign culture who doesn't have enough 
      American cultural "exposure" to get the "in" references

   -- a news admin trying to convince a real administrator wearing
      a suit that no, News isn't something we put together just to
      pull your leg, and yes, appearances notwithstanding, this is
      a valuable resource we really ought to keep sinking 
      incremental resources into....

For example, for many people the following summaries are probably
somewhat suboptimal (not that they aren't cute, they just aren't
real helpful/descriptive when viewed from the perspectives previously
mentioned):

 alt.banjo            Someone's in the kitchen with Dinah.
 alt.fan.jen-coolest  Gosh, isn't she just wonderful?
 alt.fan.mst3k        I thought people hated jerks who talked in theatres.
 alt.fax              Like comp.dcom.fax, only different.
 alt.non.sequitur     Richard Nixon.
 alt.punk             Burning them keeps insects away.
 alt.religion.kibology  He's Fred, Jim.

I think the "gigabytes of copyright violation" descriptions need to
be viewed in the same sardonic context, but these descriptions should
nonetheless probably be reviewed and perhaps adjusted. 

I know, I know, this is alt we're talking about. But remember that 
many new news users see alt first (if they have an alphabetically
sorted .newsrc file), and many may cease paying attention to putative 
newsgroup descriptions for all newsgroups based on what they are given
in the way of descriptions for selected alt hierarchy groups.

There is also a pragmatic issue here:

After all, the "gigabytes of copyright violations" really does suggest 
a rather easily defended line of attack to those who'd rather not carry 
particular groups from the alt.binaries.pictures hierarchy. 

Not a flame, just a suggestion that these things be viewed in context
and that maybe the quasi-canonical newsgroups file UUNET distributes 
could possibly do with a bit of a review and an edit rather than being 
taken en bloc/unedited.

Regards, and Seasons Greetings,

Joe St Sauver (joe@oregon.uoregon.edu)
