Finals are winding down and people are scattering to the four winds
to be home for the holidays.  I've been making some great progress
on Kerberos and UofI Direct, much to some people's relief.  It leaves
some time to reflect and ponder.  Today I was musing what would be the
proper reward for network cretinism for what did I find in my email today
but

Dear Friend,
                My name is Dave Rhodes. In September 1988 my car
was repossessed and the bill collectors were hounding me like you
wouldn't believe. I was laid off and my unemployment checks had
run out. The only escape I had from the pressure of failure was
my computer and my modem. I longed to turn my avocation into my
vocation. [200 lines of chain letter deleted]
           ============================================


When I ran an Explorer Scout post at Tektronix I kept a length of
lead filled steel pipe on my desk as a symbol of authority.  At the
beginning of each year I would explain to both new and old members
the facts of life while caressing my symbol.  Now and then I wish I
had kept it for occasional use here.

I haven't kept accurate numbers but this feels right:

	Every cretin is worth 100 messages to postmaster.

This is a more statistical law than most.  A chain-mail post will get
me between 50 and 150 depending on how many groups the cretin used.
A "Faggots must Die" message to alt.sex.* and soc.motss is good for
about 200.  I imagine Serdar Argic in his prime probably got 100 a
day.  I used to answer these with a canned response but found even
that took up too much time.

My other law, the first actually, was coined some years back and is
more philosophical.

	With computers, you can never solve just one problem.

Chances are you'll have to solve four others before getting to the
original problem.

Oh well, back to the salt mines.

/pbp
