>the world are going to conclude that the financial risk (i.e. libel 
>awards and legal costs) of giving net access to their students or 
>employees makes continued net access just not worthwhile.
>They will therefore, logically, remove net access from their sites.
[...]

In the U.S. the precedents are already being set for the good:
computer service providers that exercise no editorial control on their
users, are not liable for their user's liable (_Cubby v. Compuserve_).
Also, universities that give their students access to a forums, but
that exercise no editial control on those students, are not liable for
their student's liable (see refs).

What would happen if the United Kingdom made the first (or every)
carrier of libel liable? I don't think the rest of the world would
stop giving students access to the Usenet. I think the rest of the
world would stop giving the UK access to Usenet.

Death-of-the-Net predictions aside, wins by Godfrey against carriers
would be bad for the UK and the world.

What are the names of these cases? Are they getting any publicity?
Is anyone fighting them on civil liberty grounds?

- Carl

