>                     THE CHRONICLE OF HIGHER EDUCATION
>         November 24, 1993 (Volume 40, Issue 14) pg. a16, 131 lines.
[...]
>     Officials at UT-Dallas said that the electrical-engineering student,
>Gregory N. Steshenko, had been warned several times that his missives to
>electronic bulletin boards on Russian and Ukrainian politics were not
>permitted under university rules. Those rules restrict the use of campus
>computers to activities that are directly related to educational purposes.
[...]

I decided to check if the U. of Texas at Dallas enforces their
"educational-purposes-only" policy consistently or if they only apply
it against those whose content draws complaints.

Today, on part of the current Usenet spool at cs.uiuc.edu, I found articles
on these topics from the U. of Texas at Dallas:

  * Comments about the an episode of the Sonic Saturday morning cartoon
  * A query about air-to-air combat by helicopters
  * A query about germinating seeds of an orange tree that an uncle
       brought back from Florida 20 years ago
  * A query about the lyric to a song by a Christian music group
  * A query about rumor than a Christian musician had been in jail
  * Some baseball statistics on Juan Gonzalez
  * A query about a rumor about U. of Alabama football coaching
  * A complaint about the amount of bickering in a sports newsgroup

I applaud the officials at the U. of Texas at Dallas for supporting
the free inquiry and free expression for their students by letting
them write on such a wide variety of topics. I condemn them for
apparently defining topics that generate complaints as
"noneducational" and, thus, forbidden.

If I was Steshenko's lawyer, I'd buy the Usenet CD-ROMs for the time
period he was posting. I believe this would provide strong evidence of
selective content-based rule enforcement by the U. of Texas at Dallas.

Here is some information on selective enforcement:

 _Public Schools Law: Teachers' and Students' Rights_
2nd Ed. by Martha M. McCarthy and Nelda H. Cambron-McCabe,
published in 1987 by Allyn and Bacon, Inc.

p. 121 "Although school boards are not obligated to support student
papers, if a given publication was originally created as a free
speech forum, removal of financial or other school board support can
be construed as an unlawful effort to stifle free expression. In
essence, school authorities cannot withdraw support from a student
publication simply because of displeasure with the content. In an
illustrative case, the Eight Circuit Court of Appeals ruled that a
university could not change its funding policy for a student paper
based on the 'hue and cry' of the public objecting to a particular
issue {78}.

{78} Stanley v. Magrath, 719 F.2d 279, 282-283 (8th Cir. 1983).


