From caf-talk Caf Dec 18 21:29:13 1993
Newsgroups: news.admin.misc,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: brad@clarinet.com (Brad Templeton)
Subject: Re: Banning "erotic" picture groups? (TIM HESTER READ THIS)
Date: Sun, 19 Dec 1993 01:43:21 GMT
Message-ID: <1993Dec19.014321.28453@clarinet.com>

You people seem to talk as though the removal of pirated stuff is a
"bad thing."   Well, it is if you like pirating, I guess.  But that's
hardly an argument as to why site admins should take the risk.

Actually, this is a hot topic in the BBS world, and not many USENET
people are aware of it.

The hard fact in the BBS world has been for some time that the one sure
way to make money as a BBS was to put up erotic image files.  Some sysops
have no problem with this, some feel uncomfortable, due to whatever moral
hangups they may have, at the prospect of becoming pornography vendors.
That's up to them, of course.

Some are also tempted over the line into doing pirate stuff, and a couple
have been shut down or paid rather stiff penalties.

At the same time, Playboy and Penthouse, realizing this, have developed
their own BBSs to tap this market, and they will get more upset at the
pirates as time goes on.   My own prediction is that sometime in the future
they will mail out orders to USENET site admins, ranging from UUNET to
major corporations and Universities telling them not to carry pirate
newsgroups.   I expect many will comply.

But you may not know that the BBS world, *already* past this point, has
developed its own answers, and there are people now selling legit CD-ROMS
of stuff that can be distributed.  I met one guy who sells these CD-ROMS.
He calls in models, shoots them nude for $50 and publishes the photos.  It's
not Playboy quality, but this market actually isn't so picky about that
quality, and he makes money at this.   There is no shortage, he says, of
attractive models willing to do nude shots for cheap in L.A.

I actually don't think moderating a group would be that hard, but not for
the reasons suggested.  While some legit stuff is coming out (well, legit
if you pay the producers) the fact is that if you see a professional
shot, you can be 99% sure it is pirate unless you're getting it from the
photographer.   (There are BBSs for amateur sex photos as well.)

By and large, while people may do sex and nude shots for cheap, they never
do it free, and top quality pro photographers also don't work for free.

So the rule the moderator has to run under is pretty simple.  You need
a note from every submitter that they own the graphic being sent in, and
no reason to doubt them.  That just leaves very little stuff.

But there is very little stuff that is not pirated, so if you bemoan
"very little stuff" you're really just saying you think it's a bad thing
for all the pirated stuff to go away, and you get no sympathy from me.

I do have one personal profit motive in this argument.  The internet and
USENET are getting a pirate reputation, and that hurts me in my efforts to
bring legitimate professional information into this community.  And it
hurts the people who want to pay me to do that.   I've been arguing against
any support of information piracy on this net for over a decade, long
before I started selling information in this community, and it turns out
that I was wise to argue that way, because a piracy reputation for the
net hurts me, hurts my subscribers, and I think it hurts a lot of other
people here too.
-- 
