Several years ago our county library successfully defended a
couple of policies: confidentiality of patron records and
refusal to use an industry rating system for library materials.

Santa Clara County Library (in the heart of Silicon Valley)
has a policy of not using any ratings to describe videos (or
other materials), and anyone with a card can check out any
item.  So a kid can check out what the public thinks of a
as an R-rated movie.  A magic store near the Milpitas branch
library attracted some kids who were carrying their library
materials, including some videos, and the magic store
owner was shocked to see them with such material.  He took
his complaint to the library board. They back the library,
as did the Board of Supervisors (over a lengthy period where
the San Jose Merc. News did not support the library in its
editorial).  So the policy still stands, and the parent can't
find out what the kids are reading.  The policy is well
publicized and parents are aware of what it means for their
children to have a card.

When I say the parent can't find out what their kids are
reading, I mean they can't get a printout of the circ.
records. Let's hope they can talk to their children and
find out that way.

