In article <CIA5z7.DC4@cs.uiuc.edu> kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:
>What I see has a bad thing (in both cases) is:
>
>   Removal of material without due process.

Agreed.  Though in fact, if you believe -- or in these cases are pretty
much sure -- that a newsgroup is in violation of laws, you may have a legal
duty to remove it immediately when you become aware of this, but there should
be a fairly rapid appeal process to question your decision at any quasi-public
site.
>   Making universities and other service providers liable for
>   material they had no editorial control over and no reasonable
>   way to know about

Also a bad principle, and if site admins are every liable for individual
articles, we are in trouble.  I do think admins can and should be
responsible for what groups they carry.  While a site admin can and
should be able to say, "I carry all of alt, but I don't look into it" that
defence only works until you are told that there are illegal groups inside
alt.  (Which you are told, in news.* groups and in the newsgroups file.)
>      Risk: Removal of newsgroups that don't need to be removed
>       (e.g. alt.binaries.pictures.erotica.d, all the alts's.).

That's just a risk of stupidity, and yes, we should educate about it.
Hit me once, when a European site deleted clari.news.law.crime.sex, a
newsgroup relating to sexual offenses, because it had the word sex in the
name.   The risk here is general stupidity, a risk everywhere.

>      Surveillance of all site postings (ala SUNY-Buffalo).
I don't see a connection between this and illegal newsgroups, unless you
mean that site admins are surveying their own posters to make sure they
do nothing illegal?  One would hope this would not be a *requirement* though
it is certainly a right of anybody to read public postings in any fashion
they see fit.  Acting to shut down non-illegal activity that is merely
"offensive" is the risk you probably refer to here.

I see many great risks in tolerating openly pirate groups, and not just
to me.  If we don't show that piracy is not tolerated, the net overall
will be hurt and limited in what it can do and what can be done with it.

More to the point, those who oppose *legal* but "offensive" traffic on the
net will have a real weapon they can use to fight the net, because by
tolerating these pirate groups, we give them a legitimate weapon we can't
argue about.

We can argue against those who ban newsgroups as offensive, and more to
the point we can fight them and win.   We can't win against those who
(with no qualms) will use the piracy complaint.  We don't have a defence
against that.

The problem is that people don't want trouble.  If USENET has illegal
sections, those who view it from afar will think that USENET itself is
a hotbed of piracy, that USENET as a whole is trouble.   The BBS world
has a hard time fighting this rap.  By far most BBSs are legit, but so
many people think of BBSs as hotbeds of kiddie porn, pickup-joints for
pedophiles, pirate software supermarkets or worse.  These things come to
mind when people say BBS.

Do you want these things to come to mind when people say USENET?
They will.  "But 99% of USENET is legit!" you'll say.

Then they will say, look at these propagation figures and readership
figures.   These groups are among the most popular on the net.  Most
go to over HALF the net.  (They won't know that due to crossposting,
you get a few extra sites in this report unless you totally delete the
group.)  But worst of all, the *legal* binary groups -- fine art, fractals,
the discussion groups -- they don't have significantly greater propagation
or readership than the illegal ones. Tell me, is the supermodel group full
of promotional shots where permission was granted for USENET distribution?
Because other than that, I know of no supermodels who do not charge for use
of their photos.

