In article <1993Dec17.103800.15201@clarinet.com>, brad@clarinet.com (Brad Templeton) writes:

> And in this case the *groups* are organized copyright infringement,
> not just the messages.     You've got a good defence against being
> sued because a message was pirate.

I know I've asked this question before and it's been ignored, but I'll ask
it again:  Which groups have been organized specifically for copyright
infingment and by whom?

> Don't forget the Kinkos case.   Kinkos just sells photocopying, but they
> let a professor come in regularly and copy copyrighted books.  Because
> the prof did it all the time, it stopped being the professor's violation
> and became Kinkos' as well.

This is an interesting point.  If a site allows a user to repeatedly post
articles that are pirated does this make the site responsible?  From the
above example I'd say this is what Brad is indicating.  Now, is every site
on the usenet responsible because a site refuses to stop a violating user
and continues to post.  Should feed sites pull connections?  Should the
net at large "censor" the postings of an offending site?

> Have you been told that these groups are mostly pirate?  Yes, both here
> and in newsgroup lists and in any other places.

Yes, but who is saying this?  People who claim to not get the group!
When I ask for hard evidence, they don't reply to my articles!

> What was your decision?

> 	a) Ignore the warning, keep the group
> 	b) Investigate the group and
> 		keep it
> 		delete it
> 	c) Delete the group

> You are responsible for your decision.

I am always responsible for all my decisions.  This goes without saying.
But the important issues are being skirted by the "you better drop these
groups" crowd.

For my own investigation of the claim that "most of the articles posted in
the binary newsgroups are pirate scans" have show the claim to be false.

In fact, most of the articles posted in the binary newsgroups aren't even
binary postings!  They are "original" text postings, usually of an
informational nature.  So the claim that "most" (> 50%) of the articles
posted are pirate scans is simply not true.  I'm not even sure that it's
true that most of the scans that are posted (if you eliminate the purely
text traffic) are pirate scans.

Some of the postings are indeed pirate scans.  But this is true in the
rest of USENET too.

My question is what percentage of the traffic constitues a concern for a
site (who is just receiving the articles, not originating them).  This
could be applied not only to the "binary" groups but to USENET in general.

If we assume zero tolerance, then a site should drop the majority of USENET.
If we don't assume zero tolerance, then what tolerance should we be accepting?

